👉 this does NOT depend on where the function is written
👉 It depends on how the function is called
🔥 Golden Rule (Never Forget)
this = the object that is calling the function

🔹 1. Global Context
console.log(this);👉 In browser → window
👉 In Node → global

🔹 2. Inside a Normal Function
function show() {
console.log(this);
}show();
👉 In browser (non-strict) → window
👉 In strict mode → undefined
🔹 3. Inside an Object (MOST IMPORTANT)
const user = {
name: "Ayush",
greet() {
console.log(this.name);
}
};user.greet(); // Ayush
👉 this = user
👉 Because user is calling greet()

🔹 4. Arrow Functions (TRICKY ⚠️)
const user = {
name: "Ayush",
greet: () => {
console.log(this.name);
}
};
user.greet(); // ❌ undefined👉 Arrow functions DO NOT have their own this
👉 They take this from outside (lexical scope)

✅ Fix:
const user = {
name: "Ayush",
greet() {
const inner = () => {
console.log(this.name);
};
inner();
}
};
user.greet(); // Ayush
🔹 5. call, apply, bind (CONTROL this)
👉 call
function greet() {
console.log(this.name);
}const user = { name: "Ayush" };
greet.call(user); // Ayush

👉 apply (same, but args in array)
greet.apply(user);
👉 bind (returns new function)
const fn = greet.bind(user);
fn(); // Ayush
🔹 6. Constructor Function (new keyword)
function User(name) {
this.name = name;
}const u1 = new User("Ayush");
console.log(u1.name); // Ayush
👉 this = newly created object

🧠 Memory Trick (Super Simple)
👉 Ask yourself:
“Who is calling the function?”

• obj.fn() → this = obj

• fn() → this = window / undefined

• Arrow → this = parent

💬 Perfect Interview Answer
“The value of this in JavaScript depends on how a function is called. In object methods, it refers to the object calling the method. In regular functions, it refers to the global object (or undefined in strict mode). Arrow functions don’t have their own this and inherit it from their lexical scope. We can also explicitly set this using call, apply, and bind.”
